      "Overview of the workshop goals, target audience, and what you'll learn about FRC programming best practices.",
    content:
      "Hardware, for real. Workshop #1 runs on a Kraken X44 motor, a ThroughBore encoder (a CANcoder inside), and a CANivore. Workshop #2 adds a swerve drivetrain and a Limelight camera. There is no version of this course you can do without them — see the note below. Software. Prerequisites No Java yet. The Java You Need , before any lesson asks you to write code. Everything before that page is readable without it. A mechanism to program. Mechanism CAD How long: real motors over a CANivore . It is not a physics model of a robot. Unplug the hardware and there is nothing to watch. Every page names the actual device — Kraken, TalonFX, CANcoder, CANivore, Phoenix 6. There are no \"if you are using a different motor controller\" branches anywhere on the site, because the workshop supplies the hardware and every team following along builds the same mechanism. If your shop runs something else, the ideas transfer and the code does not. Which version of FRC's libraries you are learning decides whether outside help is any use to you. FRC programming changed shape for 2027, and this site teaches the new shape: WPILib 2027 alpha Commands v3 and OpModes . Mechanisms, commands, and triggers, with each robot mode written as its own class. Java 25 org.wpilib SystemCore — the robot controller your code deploys to. CTRE Phoenix 6 , also an alpha release, for every motor and sensor. RobotContainer , import edu.wpi.first Project Setup The same list lives in the sidebar, and the arrows at the bottom of every page walk it in order. Lessons marked optional are side trips — skip one and the next lesson still works. That address is left over from an older version of the course. The page does not teach PathPlanner, and this stack does not use PathPlanner anywhere — autonomous routines drive to a pose with CTRE's own path code, which you build yourself in the two lessons before it. The URL stays so links posted to Discord and printed on slides keep working. This is written for FRC students, most of them in middle or high school, who have never programmed anything. It is also written for the mentor or lead programmer sitting next to them, which is why the pages say what the code does rather than only what to type. Team 5712, Hemlock's Gray Matter, built this site with help from its friends. Two goals shaped it. The first is that you learn the patterns good FRC teams already use, instead of inventing your own and finding out in week five. The second is that the code holds up at a competition — where a match lasts two and a half minutes, nobody can reach the robot, and the thing that breaks is never the thing you tested. var(--fg) -0.01em var(--fg-mute) lessons.ts workshop1 Workshop #1 — an arm and a flywheel Wire it, check it turns, then write the code that drives it. By the end you hold a button and the arm goes to an angle you chose and stops there. workshop2 Workshop #2 — a swerve drive that knows where it is A drivetrain, a log file, a camera, and a robot that drives itself to a point on the field without a driver touching the sticks. advanced Advanced Topics — the other way to write commands Everything above is written in one style. These three pages teach the second one and the two places it earns its keep. A motor that does nothing, then a robot that finds its own way across the field finds its own way This site is the written version of Team 5712 /prerequisites /java-basics /mechanism-cad about an hour Nothing here assumes you have written code before. It does assume the hardware is in front of you. Every lesson ends with something you can hear and watch move, which is the whole reason the course is shaped this way. Read the pages in order. Each one leaves you with code that runs, and the next one changes that same code. alert-info WHAT YOU the-hardware-is-the-course The hardware is the course alert-warning WATCH OUT CTRE hardware, and nothing else what-you-are-learning-exactly What you are learning, exactly NOTE · API STATUS Alpha software moves /project-setup the-road-ahead The road ahead var(--accent) 0.08em uppercase ONE ODD URL Autonomous: Driving to a Pose lives at /pathplanner who-this-is-for-and-who Who this is for, and who made it",
