    content:
      "Hardware Setup , which is the first page that asks you to plug something in. What you'll finish with: every program installed, and an honest answer to whether you have the hardware to follow along. About an hour , most of it waiting on downloads. Software Requirements WPILib Game Tools Install VS Code and National Instruments Game Tools. On this stack you only need Game Tools for the Driver Station , the program that enables and disables the robot. Phoenix Tuner X TalonFX and other CTRE hardware. AdvantageScope A viewer for robot logs and data. You'll need it for debugging and tuning. A lite version comes preinstalled with WPILib, but we recommend downloading the latest full version from the GitHub releases page. Git Use Git for version control. Summarize changes clearly (e.g., 'Add drivetrain PID tuning logic'). WPILib 2027 Alpha stack (2027-Template) This workshop runs on the WPILib 2027 alpha Java 25 SystemCore , the robot's onboard computer. Start from the team's 2027-Template, a ready-made robot project you copy. One catch: pick the 2027-dev main Project Setup , so there is nothing to download yet. Elastic Dashboard (optional for Workshop #1) Logging @Teleop(name = \"Teleop\") @Autonomous(name = \"Drive To Pose\") — and those names are what the driver sees on the Driver Station. The driver picks one there, and that is the only one the robot builds. Java — nothing to install, and nothing to learn first You do not need to know Java before you start. You do need to read it. Robot code on this site is Java from the first lesson that has code in it, and it does not stay at the beginner end for long — lambdas, method references, and class declarations all show up in Workshop #1. The Java You Need Do not go take a Java course first. Read that page when you get to it. Hardware Requirements Mechanism Setup Running Your Code PID Control . None of that works without the hardware in front of you. Workshop #1 runs your code through something WPILib calls Hardware Simulation, and the name misleads people. What is simulated is the robot controller: your code runs on your laptop instead of on a SystemCore. The motor commands are real. They go out over the CANivore to an actual Kraken, which actually spins. , ten lessons later. The minimum to follow Workshop #1 The assembled mechanism. Mechanism CAD , built and wired. Its bill of materials is the exact parts list, down to the battery cable and the CAN terminating resistor. An existing mechanism off an old robot works too, as long as it runs on the hardware below. A Kraken X44. The motor your code commands, with its A CANcoder. The WCP ThroughBore encoder is a CANcoder inside, which is why the code calls it one. It reports the arm's real angle and remembers that angle after a power cycle. The arm build uses one; the flywheel build does not. A CANivore. Plugs into a USB port on your laptop and runs the CAN bus that the motor and encoder sit on. This is the part that stands in for a robot controller all through Workshop #1. Name yours canivore — the mechanism code on every Workshop #1 branch looks for that exact name. A charged battery and its cable. An Xbox-style controller , plugged into the same laptop. Every button binding in Workshop #1 is written against the first controller on the driver station. No SystemCore, no radio, no robot. Workshop #1 runs from a laptop, a CANivore, and one mechanism on a table. Workshop #2 moves to a swerve drivetrain, which is a different pile of hardware. Ready to Start? , where you plug the CANivore in and find every device in Tuner X. text-[var(--accent)] underline hover:no-underline text-[var(--accent)] Two lists: what to install, and what to have on the bench what to have on the bench The software half goes on your laptop. Set aside an hour for downloads and installers, longer on a slow connection. The hardware half sits in front of you. From Hardware Setup on, every page in Workshop #1 assumes a real motor you can power up and watch turn. You need both halves. There is no software-only path through Workshop #1. alert-info BEFORE YOU START What this page is /hardware _blank noopener noreferrer wpilib motor controller /project-setup /logging-implementation A dashboard does not pick which mode runs /java-basics /mechanism-setup /running-program /pid-control alert-warning READ THIS FIRST Hardware simulation is not a physics simulation /mechanism-cad alert-tip What you do not need",
